Refactor this code by avoiding @lru_cache on a method that holds a mutable self:

class PriceBook:
    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)
    def price(self, sku):
        return db.lookup_price(sku)

output:
from functools import lru_cache

class PriceBook:
    def __init__(self, db):
        self._db = db
        self._prices = lru_cache(maxsize=None)(self._lookup)
    def _lookup(self, sku):
        return self._db.lookup_price(sku)
    def price(self, sku):
        return self._prices(sku)

What changed: decorating a method with @lru_cache keys on `self`, pinning instances in memory forever; holding the cache on the instance keeps lifetimes tied to the object.

